  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nAn upper level disturbance traversing the northern plains will\npush a weak cold front through western Kansas Saturday, serving \nto delay the next hot spell by a day. However, given the \npersistent upper level ridging over the southern plains, the \nheat will build back across the high plains by Sunday and \nMonday. Record highs are near 100 both days; and these could be\ntied or broken. It may be a little cooler (but more humid) near\nor just north of the warm front around Hays and WaKeeney.\n\nA stronger upper level disturbance will traverse the northern \nplains early next week and push a cold front into Kansas by \nMonday evening. This front is expected to become stationary near\nthe KS/OK state line by Tuesday and Wednesday. With abundant \nlow level moisture on the cool side of the front, t-storm \nchances are moderate to high from Monday evening through \nWednesday. The strongest signal for beneficial rain is across \ncentral Kansas, including locations such as Hays, Larned and La\nCrosse. Despite the favorable pattern, the grand ensemble still\nshows only modest (30-40%) chances for .5\" or greater of rain, \neven across central Kansas. However, as we have seen over the\npast week, much larger rain amounts can occur when several\nt-storms move across the same area, with much smaller amounts\nelsewhere.",
